chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 4 of the 3rd edition teaches functions: the def statement that defines one and the call that runs it, arguments passed into parameters, return values and the None value, named parameters such as print's sep and end, the call stack that remembers where each call returns to, the local and the global scope and four rules for telling them apart, the global statement, and try and except for recovering from an error, closing with two short animation programs." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "One printed output of the chapter has changed: it shows UnboundLocalError: local variable 'eggs' referenced before assignment, while Python 3.14.4 says cannot access local variable 'eggs' where it is not associated with a value, the wording the Programming FAQ now prints; the class, its parent NameError and the cause are unchanged, and the chapter's other printed error messages, NameError and ZeroDivisionError, are the same under 3.14.4." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P04, chx:P08 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's copy-and-paste listing, which it shows to argue for functions, is not equivalent to the function version it stands for: its last line is print(' Good evening!') with a leading space, so running the two programs gives outputs that differ in exactly that line - which is the maintenance hazard of duplicated code the passage warns about." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The reference documentation explains why the chapter's fourth scope rule and its UnboundLocalError follow from one fact: which names are local is decided by scanning the whole function body for name-binding operations, so an assignment anywhere in a function makes the name local everywhere in it; the standard library's symtable module shows the decision - the same name eggs is global in a function with a global statement, local in one that assigns it, and global in one that only reads it." ; sen0414:cites chx:P03, chx:P05, chx:P04 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Beyond the chapter, a function's parameters can be positional-only, with a slash, or keyword-only, with a star, and calling them the wrong way raises a TypeError that names the problem; a default argument value is evaluated once, so a mutable default such as an empty list is shared between calls and the tutorial's remedy is a default of None; and nonlocal lets an inner function rebind a name of the enclosing function, the middle case between local and global that the chapter does not cover." ; sen0414:cites chx:P02, chx:P13, chx:P14, chx:P15, chx:P05 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.14 changed two things a course on functions meets: annotations on a function are no longer evaluated when it is defined, so a function annotated with names that do not exist yet can be defined and called, and reading its annotations raises NameError until they are evaluated on request; and except may list several exception types without brackets when no as clause is used, which 3.13 and earlier reject." ; sen0414:cites chx:P10, chx:P11, chx:P12 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's call-stack example reproduces line for line, and the stack itself can be looked at from the program: the standard library's traceback module lists the frames a call is running inside, and the interpreter refuses to grow the stack without end - the recursion limit is 1000 and a function that calls itself forever raises RecursionError, a subclass of RuntimeError, rather than crashing." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P06, chx:P08 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 4 is best taught as name resolution seen from the function: a call creates a scope, the compiler decides which names in it are local, the call stack holds the scopes that are waiting, and try and except decide where in that stack an error is handled; the book's rules are the four cases of one lookup rule, the printed messages should be regenerated under the Python in use, and parameters, defaults, nonlocal, annotations and the 3.14 except syntax are what the chapter leaves for the course to add." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P03, chx:P10 .
chx:F9 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Terms used without definition"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter text names tools and ideas that the earlier corpus did not explain: reserved keywords, the lambda expression, the nested function, the function taken from a module, the positional argument, the frame object and stack inspection, recursion, namespaces and the lookup of names, the symbol table, the nonlocal statement, the exception object, the traceback, the exception hierarchy, the time.sleep function, the KeyboardInterrupt exception, the sys.exit function, the two practice programs, function annotations, function naming and comments. The Python glossary defines the namespace, the nested scope, the lambda, the keyword argument and the positional argument; the language reference gives the function definition, the try statement and the lambda expression, the call, and the function object, the frame object and NoneType; the library documentation covers traceback, inspect, symtable, time, random, keyword, the constant None and annotationlib; the tutorial introduction defines the comment; PEP 3107 defines function annotations and PEP 8 gives the conventions for names and comments. Every output and error message quoted was executed under Python 3.12.11 and 3.14.4, and the messages that differ between releases - division by zero, the empty range of randint, the unexpected keyword of print, the except clause without brackets, deferred annotations and the annotation blocks in the symbol table - are pinned to their release in the corpus and never quoted as stable. Two statements of the chapter do not hold when run: its hint to print the Collatz values with sep puts each value on its own line, and its claim that the sequence reaches 1 for any integer fails for 0 and for -1." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P17, chx:P18, chx:P19, chx:P20, chx:P21, chx:P22, chx:P23, chx:P24, chx:P25, chx:P26, chx:P27, chx:P28, chx:P29, chx:P30, chx:P31 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "37 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "31 sources: P01 to P16 opened and verified at 2026-09-28T23:09:18, P17 to P31 at 2026-10-01T19:39:44 to 19:39:50; 28 cited by findings; the rest consulted but carrying no claim yet." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
